1276 0 obj
<<
/Title (Next, we performed another hypothesis test to answer an important business question using the cruise dataset. The main question we investigated was:)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1274 0 R
/Next 1278 0 R
>>
endobj
1277 0 obj
<<
/Title (* Null Hypothesis \(H0\): All cruise companies have the same average cruise duration. * Alternative Hypothesis \(H1\): At least one company has a different average cruise duration.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1279 0 R
/Next 1275 0 R
>>
endobj
1278 0 obj
<<
/Title (Do longer cruises generally cost more, or is there no real relationship between duration and price?)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1276 0 R
/Next 1280 0 R
>>
endobj
1279 0 obj
<<
/Title (We set up the hypotheses as follows:)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1281 0 R
/Next 1277 0 R
>>
endobj
1280 0 obj
<<
/Title (To answer this, we used a statistical method called the Pearson Correlation Test. This test is used when we want to measure the strength and direction of the relationship between two numeric variables. In our case, the two variables were cruise duration and cruise price.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1278 0 R
/Next 1282 0 R
>>
endobj
1281 0 obj
<<
/Title (To answer this question, we used a One-Way ANOVA test. This statistical method is appropriate when comparing the average value of one numeric variable across more than two groups. In this case, the groups were the 9 cruise companies, and the numeric variable was cruise duration measured in nights.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1283 0 R
/Next 1279 0 R
>>
endobj
1282 0 obj
<<
/Title (First, we set up two hypotheses:)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1280 0 R
/Next 1284 0 R
>>
endobj
1283 0 obj
<<
/Title (For the third hypothesis test, we wanted to determine whether different cruise companies offer different average cruise durations. In other words, we tested whether all companies provide trips with similar lengths, or whether some companies focus on shorter cruises while others specialize in longer voyages.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1285 0 R
/Next 1281 0 R
>>
endobj
1284 0 obj
<<
/Title (* Null Hypothesis \(H0\): There is no relationship between cruise duration and price. * Alternative Hypothesis \(H1\): There is a significant relationship between cruise duration and price.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1282 0 R
/Next 1286 0 R
>>
endobj
1285 0 obj
<<
/Title (This analysis is useful because it helps us understand pricing factors in the cruise industry. It shows that trip length has a major impact on the final cruise price, which can help companies improve pricing strategies and help customers better understand what affects travel costs.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1287 0 R
/Next 1283 0 R
>>
endobj
1286 0 obj
<<
/Title (Then we ran the correlation test on the cleaned dataset. The result gave us a correlation coefficient of 0.7877 and a p-value of 0.0, which means the probability that this relationship happened by chance is extremely small.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1284 0 R
/Next 1288 0 R
>>
endobj
1287 0 obj
<<
/Title (This means that there is a strong positive relationship between cruise duration and price. In other words, longer cruises usually cost more than shorter cruises.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1288 0 R
/Next 1285 0 R
>>
endobj
1288 0 obj
<<
/Title (Because the p-value was less than 0.05, we rejected the null hypothesis.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1286 0 R
/Next 1287 0 R
>>
endobj
